<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp</h1><p class="st-meta">1368 CE – 1459 CE (approx.) · capital Słupsk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Pomeranian duchy ruled from Stolp, whose prince Eric of Pomerania became king of the Kalmar Union.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1368 a new partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast">Pomerania-Wolgast</a> gave the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Further Pomerania</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swina">Świna</a> and Oder, to Duke Bogislaw V, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> (Słupsk). His dynasty kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-pomerania">Elizabeth</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Further Pomerania</a>, a land of farms, forests and small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Rügenwalde</a>. Its dukes balanced between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, grandson of Bogislaw V, was elected king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> in 1397.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Deposed from his Scandinavian thrones, Eric retired first to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gotland">Gotland</a> and from 1449 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Rügenwalde</a> (Darłowo), where he ruled part of the duchy until his death in 1459. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-ii-duke-of-pomerania">Eric II of Pomerania-Wolgast</a>, and all the Pomeranian lands were later reunited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-x">Bogislaw X</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-pomerania-stolp.jpg" alt="Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp" width="320" height="204"><figcaption>Image: J a1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grunwald_S%C5%82upsk_i_Szczecin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1368 CE – 1459 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Słupsk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bogislaw V, Wartislaw VII, Eric of Pomerania</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German, Kashubian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pommern-Stolp, Duchy of Słupsk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1414&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerania-stolp" aria-label="Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5056 312 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5376 568L5305 579M5376 568L5324 597"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5305 579h0M5324 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5376 568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5376 568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Słupsk. <a href="/atlas?year=1414&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerania-stolp">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
